cff-version: 1.2.0
title: perimeter
message: "If you use this work, please cite it."
authors:
  - family-names: Kelly-Reif
    given-names: Chelsea
# `version` mirrors the in-development version in pyproject.toml, which is the single
# source of truth; tests/test_release_claims.py asserts the two agree.
#
# `date-released` is deliberately absent. It carried 2026-08-07 with no tag, no signed
# tag and no published artifact, which the README's Release & Versioning row said in the
# same tree. A citation manager prints that field as the date the software was released,
# so the file published a release date for a release that never happened; the date was
# real, it was just the date of something else. The same test ties its presence to a tag
# naming the declared version, in both directions, so it comes back when a release is
# actually cut and cannot be left out of one.
version: 0.1.0
license: Apache-2.0
